---
// REVIEW: Layer 2 interpretation for the Fatigue Severity Scale, drafted for
// Craig's review. FAQ answers come from fss.content.ts and are shown word for
// word (they also feed the page's FAQPage JSON-LD).
import { FAQ, REFERENCE, SOURCES } from '../../tools/fss.content';
import '../../styles/interpretation.css';
const s = SOURCES;
---

<section class="interpretation" aria-labelledby="understanding-fss">
	<h2 id="understanding-fss">Understanding your FSS score</h2>

	<h3 id="what-it-measures">What the score measures</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.measures.a}</p>
	<p>
		It was developed in multiple sclerosis and lupus (<a href={s.krupp.href} rel="noopener">{s.krupp.label}</a>) and
		later checked in more than 1,300 people in Switzerland (<a href={s.valko.href} rel="noopener">{s.valko.label}</a>).
	</p>

	<h3 id="score-bands">FSS score interpretation</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.normal.a}</p>
	<table class="interp-table">
		<caption>Average FSS scores in validation studies</caption>
		<thead>
			<tr><th scope="col">Group</th><th scope="col">Average</th><th scope="col">Scoring 4 or more</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			{REFERENCE.map(r => <tr><th scope="row">{r.group}</th><td>{r.mean}</td><td>{r.over4}</td></tr>)}
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p>{FAQ.score5.a}</p>
	<p>
		Your result is shown to one decimal place and judged on that number, so 4.0 counts as 4 or more.
	</p>

	<h3 id="change">What a change in score means</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.change.a}</p>
	<p>
		The questions cover the past week, so one bad week can move your score; a monthly trend says more than any single
		pair. The multiple sclerosis figures come from <a href={s.rooney.href} rel="noopener">{s.rooney.label}</a>.
	</p>

	<h3 id="what-it-does-not-measure">What it does not measure</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.diagnosis.a}</p>
	<p>
		For worsening after effort, see the <a href="/tools/dsq-pem/">DSQ-PEM</a> and the
		<a href="/evidence/post-exertional-malaise/">post-exertional malaise explainer</a>. The
		<a href="/tools/chalder-fatigue-scale/">Chalder Fatigue Scale</a> scores the fatigue itself.
	</p>

	<h3 id="taking-it-to-your-gp">Taking this to your GP or consultant</h3>
	<p>
		Bring the score, the date, the trend from your saved results, and a note of which statements you rated 6 or 7. Describe
		it as a nine-question measure of how much fatigue interferes with daily life, developed in multiple sclerosis and
		now one of the most commonly used fatigue questionnaires. It can show that fatigue is substantial and how it has
		changed. It cannot show the cause, and it is not a test for ME/CFS or long COVID. See
		<a href="/testing/preparing-for-gp-appointment/">preparing for a GP appointment</a>.
	</p>

	<div class="tool-foot">
		<slot name="disclaimer" />
		<details class="sources" id="sources">
			<summary>Sources and licence</summary>
			<slot name="citation" />
			<ul class="interp-sources">
				<li>Krupp LB, LaRocca NG, Muir-Nash J, Steinberg AD. The fatigue severity scale: application to patients with multiple sclerosis and systemic lupus erythematosus. <em>Arch Neurol</em> 1989;46(10):1121–1123. <a href={s.krupp.href} rel="noopener">Article</a></li>
				<li>Valko PO, Bassetti CL, Bloch KE, Held U, Baumann CR. Validation of the fatigue severity scale in a Swiss cohort. <em>Sleep</em> 2008;31(11):1601–1607. <a href={s.valko.href} rel="noopener">Full text</a></li>
				<li>Rooney S, McFadyen DA, Wood DL, Moffat DF, Paul PL. Minimally important difference of the fatigue severity scale and modified fatigue impact scale in people with multiple sclerosis. <em>Mult Scler Relat Disord</em> 2019. <a href={s.rooney.href} rel="noopener">Article</a></li>
			</ul>
		</details>
	</div>
</section>
